<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Capitol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, is the seat of the US Congress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-thornton">William Thornton</a>, a physician born in the British Virgin Islands, won the design competition, and construction began in 1793. British troops burned the building in 1814, and it was later enlarged with new wings and a great cast-iron dome completed in 1866.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol.jpg" alt="United States Capitol" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Capitol_east_side.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small planter society grew sugar and cotton with enslaved labour; an 18th-century Quaker community on Tortola and Jost Van Dyke produced John Coakley Lettsom and William Thornton, architect of the <b>United States Capitol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a></li></ul></section>
